Object.assign(window.LESSON_CONTENT || (window.LESSON_CONTENT = {}), {"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w1":{"overview":"Students will identify a user need and apply agreed classroom and workshop routines. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will analyse an everyday product using evidence about function, user needs and material choices. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"25 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to identify a user need and apply agreed classroom and workshop routines.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display two pencil holders, one stable and one easily tipped. Ask which better serves a student and what evidence would help decide. Separate a user problem from a preferred colour or shape."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce designing as responding to a user, purpose and context. Explain the cycle of investigating, proposing, modelling, testing and improving. Establish equipment checks, clear work areas, permission before tool use and reporting damage; this term begins with paper models and teacher-prepared components."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a brief observation of a crowded desk. Record the user, the difficulty reaching stationery and a possible design need without jumping to a finished solution. Demonstrate safe collection, carrying and return of the materials permitted for today."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Pairs examine a teacher-prepared desk scenario, record two observations and write a user-need statement. They identify the routines needed for a small card modelling task, organise their workspace and explain how each routine helps the group work safely and efficiently."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the need statements and ask students to distinguish evidence from an assumption about the user. Check that each student can describe what to do when equipment is damaged or an unfamiliar tool is needed."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a user-purpose-problem grid and illustrated routines. Model one observation and allow an oral explanation before writing. Give roles so both partners contribute to the investigation."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a second user with a conflicting need and explain what further research would be needed before choosing a solution."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"26 August 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to analyse an everyday product using evidence about function, user needs and material choices.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a reusable bottle and ask whether calling it good design is enough. Invite students to name a feature that can be observed or tested rather than offering a personal preference."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain product analysis through function, user, ergonomics, materials, construction, appearance, cost and environmental considerations. Distinguish an observed feature from a proposed reason for it. Explain that analysis should relate choices to the intended use."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Annotate a bottle lid, grip and base. Connect the lid seal to preventing leaks and the grip to handling, noting that suitability for a particular hand size requires user evidence. Model a simple non-destructive stability check and record its limitations."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students analyse a safe everyday product such as a pencil case or stationery organiser. They sketch and label three features, connect each to a user need and propose one improvement. Partners challenge one unsupported claim and students revise it using observations or a suggested test."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to justify one feature using the pattern feature, evidence and user benefit. Check that an improvement addresses a stated difficulty and does not simply add decoration."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Offer a labelled product image, an analysis checklist and sentence starters. Limit the first analysis to function, grip and materials before adding the remaining categories."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain a trade-off introduced by their improvement, such as greater capacity increasing size, and propose a fair way to test whether the change is worthwhile."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.13 Contexts which inform design outcomes; 1.17 Developing and communicating design ideas","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w2":{"overview":"Students will explain how emerging technologies can change design and manufacture. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will evaluate a technology using benefits, limitations and stakeholder needs. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"1 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain how emerging technologies can change design and manufacture.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare an individually cut card shape with a sheet of identical computer-cut shapes. Ask what changes when production must make many consistent items rather than one model."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Use the book’s new and emerging technologies section to introduce automation, computer-aided design and manufacture, and additive manufacture. Explain that a digital design is information while manufacture produces a physical object. Discuss effects on consistency, skills and the way work is organised."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a small manufacturer comparing manual cutting with a computer-controlled process. Trace design file, machine instructions, material and output. Identify an advantage in repeatability alongside setup cost and the need for trained people to maintain and check the process."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Groups study teacher-prepared cases of automated packaging and 3D-printed prototypes. For each they identify what changed, who is affected and a possible benefit or limitation. Students then independently explain one change using a specific stage of the manufacturing process."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish CAD from CAM and explain why automation does not remove the need for quality checks. Collect a case response to check that the claimed effect follows from the technology described."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a process diagram and vocabulary bank. Use one familiar school-production example before moving to the manufacturing cases, and support students in separating the machine from its digital instructions."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare suitability for a one-off prototype and a repeated production run, explaining why the same technology may not be the best choice in both situations."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"2 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to evaluate a technology using benefits, limitations and stakeholder needs.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Return to the automated production case and ask whether faster production automatically makes a change desirable for everyone. List the people whose experience might differ."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain evaluation as judging against criteria using evidence. Introduce effects on workers, manufacturers, customers and the environment. Discuss initial investment, training, accuracy, waste and repair, distinguishing a supported benefit from a promotional claim."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Use a fictional school deciding whether to outsource a small batch of display parts. Compare two processes against quantity, lead time, material waste and cost using supplied case data. Model a recommendation that acknowledges a drawback and states an assumption."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students use a decision grid to compare two manufacturing options for a defined brief. They identify two benefits, two limitations and the people affected, then recommend an option in a short paragraph. Partners check that the recommendation uses the supplied evidence rather than a general claim that newer is always better."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask which change in the brief could reverse the recommendation. Check that students weigh competing criteria and recognise uncertainty in the case data."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a partly completed grid and explicit stakeholder cards. Rehearse a recommendation orally using evidence, judgement and limitation prompts before students write independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify the most influential criterion, explain a sensitivity to quantity or budget and describe the additional evidence needed for a stronger decision."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.1 The impact of new and emerging technologies; 1.2 Critical evaluation","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w3":{"overview":"Students will explain environmental, social and economic challenges in product design. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will apply a lifecycle perspective to identify and justify a product improvement. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"8 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain environmental, social and economic challenges in product design.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display a low-cost disposable item and a repairable alternative. Ask what the purchase price leaves out when judging their overall impact."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce environmental effects such as resource extraction and waste, social issues such as working conditions and accessibility, and economic issues such as production cost and affordability. Explain that these considerations can conflict and that material labels alone do not establish a product’s overall impact."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Analyse a fictional desk lamp with a replaceable light source and a sealed alternative. Model how repair access may extend use but introduce extra parts or assembly costs. Separate the evidence given in the case from assumptions that would require supplier information."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Groups sort product-case statements into environmental, social and economic considerations, allowing justified overlap. Individually students propose two improvements to a classroom product and explain one likely benefit and one possible drawback for each."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain one conflict between affordability and durability without assuming all cheaper products are poor quality. Check the proposed improvements for a clear connection to the problem identified."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide category examples and a benefit-drawback organiser. Use a familiar product and highlight relevant case evidence so students can explain a concrete issue before using wider terminology."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a hidden consequence of an improvement and propose evidence that would help compare the original product with the revised version."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"9 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to apply a lifecycle perspective to identify and justify a product improvement.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask where a reusable bottle’s story begins and ends. Challenge an answer that starts at purchase or stops when the owner no longer wants it."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the stages of raw material extraction, processing, manufacture, transport, use and end of life. Introduce reducing, reusing, repairing and recycling as possible interventions. Clarify that a classroom lifecycle map identifies likely impacts and is not a quantified lifecycle assessment."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Draw a lifecycle map for a bottle using a fictional supply chain. Mark energy use during manufacture, transport distance, washing during use and material separation at disposal. Model how longer use can change the comparison and why recycling depends on local collection and separation."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students construct a lifecycle map for a selected stationery product using supplied information. They mark likely impacts at each stage and select one feasible improvement. They explain its expected effect, any trade-off and the information still needed to judge the overall result."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to trace how one decision affects more than one stage. Check that they do not claim a precise carbon saving without data or treat recyclable as a guarantee that recycling happens."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide ordered lifecycle cards, a template and one completed stage. Let students annotate a diagram before writing a linked explanation of their chosen intervention."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two improvement options across the lifecycle and explain how product lifespan or local disposal arrangements might alter their recommendation."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.14 Environmental, social and economic challenges; 1.1 Sustainability","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w4":{"overview":"Students will distinguish energy generation and storage and explain examples used in products. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will compare energy options against the needs of a defined product. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"15 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to distinguish energy generation and storage and explain examples used in products.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show pictures of a solar panel, battery and electric motor. Ask which converts incoming energy, which stores it and which produces movement."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Use the book’s energy section to distinguish renewable and finite energy resources and generation from storage. Explain that energy is transferred or converted rather than created. A battery stores energy chemically; a solar cell converts light to electrical energy, and a motor converts electrical energy into movement with losses."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Trace a solar garden light from sunlight through its panel, rechargeable battery and lamp. Model a simple energy-flow diagram and discuss what happens at night or after several cloudy days. Distinguish the panel’s role from the battery’s role."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students classify supplied energy-source and storage examples, then draw energy-flow diagrams for a torch and a solar light. They annotate one limitation of each arrangement and explain why a storage component may be useful when the source is intermittent."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students why adding a battery does not generate a new energy supply. Check that diagrams show sensible transfers and that renewable does not mean free of manufacturing or disposal impacts."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide labelled component cards and arrows. Complete the torch example together and offer a generation-storage-output word bank for the independent diagram."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain how low output, charging opportunities and product operating time influence the suitability of an energy arrangement without assuming a larger battery is always better."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"16 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to compare energy options against the needs of a defined product.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a portable reading light used where charging is sometimes unavailable. Ask what the designer needs to know before choosing its energy arrangement."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain selection criteria including portability, operating time, charging access, maintenance, purchase cost and end-of-life handling. Distinguish a primary cell from a rechargeable cell and discuss using the specified charging system. Classroom comparisons use case information; no cells are opened or improvised charging attempted."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Compare a replaceable-cell light, USB-rechargeable light and solar-rechargeable light against a supplied user scenario. Model a justified choice using operating conditions and acknowledge the weakness of the chosen option in a different setting."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete a comparison matrix using teacher-prepared product data. They recommend an arrangement for a reading light, justify it against three needs and identify one condition that would require a different solution. Partners check whether each claim is supported by the data provided."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the recommendation and ask which user requirement mattered most. Check that students distinguish energy storage capacity from generation and recognise the limits of a comparison without complete performance data."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Use a matrix with clear criteria and two options initially. Highlight relevant case facts and provide a recommendation frame linking choice, user condition and limitation."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students suggest a fair test of operating time, controlling brightness and starting condition, and explain how measured results could change their recommendation."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.3 How energy is generated and stored","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w5":{"overview":"Students will distinguish modern, smart and composite materials through their behaviour and structure. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will select a material for a defined purpose using properties and constraints. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"22 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to distinguish modern, smart and composite materials through their behaviour and structure.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show an image of a colour-changing strip and a layered sheet. Ask whether both are called smart for the same reason or whether they illustrate different material ideas."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that smart materials respond to a stimulus such as temperature or light; modern materials are developed for improved properties; composites combine constituents to achieve useful properties. Discuss thermochromic pigment and a fibre-reinforced composite, noting that categories can overlap and each material has limitations."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Use the book examples to trace stimulus and response for a thermochromic indicator. Then label fibres and matrix in a composite diagram and explain their different roles. Model why naming a material without explaining its behaviour does not justify its selection."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students sort illustrated material case cards, giving a reason for each classification. They annotate a stimulus-response example and a composite structure, then connect one property to a product use. All unfamiliar material behaviour is explored through images or teacher-prepared samples."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask what makes the indicator smart and why a composite is more than a single homogeneous substance. Check classifications through explanations rather than accepting labels alone."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide paired examples, a stimulus-response arrow diagram and a labelled composite outline. Rehearse the reason for one category together before students classify a new case."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students explain why a smart response may be useful but unsuitable for some conditions, and compare that limitation with a conventional alternative."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"23 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to select a material for a defined purpose using properties and constraints.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a reusable protective case for a small electronic item. Ask which matters more: being very rigid, absorbing impact or allowing access, and why the answer depends on the brief."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain material selection through required properties, manufacturing method, availability, cost and environmental considerations. Distinguish strength, stiffness, toughness and hardness. Emphasise that a material family name does not establish the exact performance of every grade or product."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Compare supplied cards for card, a flexible polymer and a fibre-reinforced composite against a protective-case brief. Model excluding an option that fails a critical requirement, then comparing the remaining options and acknowledging cost or repair limitations."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students use a property matrix to choose a material for a defined product component. They cite supplied evidence for three requirements, explain one rejected option and suggest a simple test to check the most important property. Partners look for an unsupported claim or a conflict with the brief."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish tough from hard using the proposed use. Check that the chosen material meets the essential requirements and that the suggested test measures the claimed property."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Offer a short glossary, pictures of the component and a partly completed matrix. Reduce the choices to two before asking students to justify the final selection independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students propose a combination of materials for different components and explain how joining or separation at end of life could complicate the design."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.4 Modern and smart materials, composite materials and technical textiles","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w6":{"overview":"Students will explain how simple mechanical devices transmit or change motion and force. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will build and test a low-risk model of a simple mechanism. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"29 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain how simple mechanical devices transmit or change motion and force.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Demonstrate a card lever using a ruler and a stable classroom pivot. Ask what changes when the effort is applied farther from the pivot while the load stays in the same place."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce input and output motion, pivot, effort and load. Use the book to compare levers, gears, pulleys and cams at an introductory level. Explain rotary, linear, reciprocating and oscillating motion, and distinguish changing speed or direction from creating energy."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a lever with labelled effort, load and pivot, then sketch two meshing gears and mark opposite rotations. Explain qualitatively how a smaller driver turning a larger driven gear reduces output speed while increasing available turning effect, with real systems subject to losses."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate three mechanism diagrams, identify their input and output and predict a direction or motion change. Pairs demonstrate one prediction using teacher-prepared card components, then individually explain how the arrangement would help a user in a simple product."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to explain why a mechanism does not provide free energy. Check motion labels and gear directions, then use an incorrect diagram to expose a common misunderstanding."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Use colour-coded input and output arrows and manipulable diagrams. Focus first on a lever and a pair of gears before comparing a cam or pulley."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two lever arrangements for the same load or explain the effect of adding an idler gear, distinguishing direction from speed ratio."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"30 September 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to build and test a low-risk model of a simple mechanism.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a finished card lever that moves a small sign and ask where it might fail. Invite predictions about the pivot position, card stiffness and unwanted rubbing."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain the modelling brief: create a manually operated card mechanism with an observable input and output. Agree success criteria for movement, stability and repeatability. Use pre-cut card and teacher-prepared holes; students use only the approved fasteners and classroom tools demonstrated."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Assemble a lever using prepared parts, label the pivot and input and test its movement. Model recording a fault such as rubbing against the base, changing one feature and retesting rather than changing several things without knowing which helped."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Pairs assemble a card mechanism, sketch its input and output and test it over repeated operations. They record one fault, make one justified change and repeat the same test. Each student writes how the mechanism works and whether evidence supports the agreed success criteria."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare the prediction, first test and revised model. Check that explanations identify the moving parts and the reason for a change, and that students return materials and report damaged components."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide pre-cut parts, numbered assembly images and a testing table. Help students locate the pivot while leaving them responsible for predicting the effect of their chosen adjustment."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two pivot positions under the same test conditions and explain the trade-off between input movement, output movement and effort."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.5 Mechanical devices","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w7":{"overview":"Students will consolidate the taught design and technology concepts through an independent mock and a structured review. They will identify misconceptions, explain corrections and apply the learning to a new question or design decision. The two lessons will make individual strengths and remaining preparation needs clear before the internal school assessment.","assessment":"Compare original mock responses, annotated corrections and new independent work. Check whether students can explain the cause of an error and transfer the corrected reasoning to another case. Record one focused preparation need for each student.","lessons":[{"date":"6 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to complete a mock assessment and identify gaps in the taught design skills.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display a short product recommendation with no evidence. Ask what a reader would need to judge it. Explain that the mock will identify which concepts and explanations require further practice."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Outline the mock coverage: user needs, product analysis, emerging technologies, lifecycle choices, energy, materials and mechanical devices. Explain the criteria for accurate terminology, labelled diagrams, evidence and justified decisions. Keep the original independent response separate from later supported corrections so progress remains visible."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Read the task instructions and evidence requirements together without solving the mock questions. Clarify how diagrams, a short product case and a planning task should be completed and submitted."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete the mock independently, including concept questions, a labelled system or mechanism diagram and a justified design decision. They mark uncertain responses and check their work against the instructions. Collect the original work and identify whether each difficulty concerns knowledge, reasoning, diagram accuracy or meeting the brief."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask each student to identify one secure skill and one uncertain answer. Use the mock evidence to select anonymous examples and group the learning needs for the next lesson."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide readable diagrams, clear instructions and agreed classroom access support. During subsequent feedback, target the specific learning need while preserving the original independent work."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify an alternative solution and explain when it would be preferable. Ask them to test whether their original justification still holds if a key user requirement changes."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"7 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use mock feedback to correct a design response and apply the learning to a new task.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show an anonymous mock error such as a reversed gear direction, a vague specification or an unsupported material choice. Ask how the error could be identified rather than simply supplying the correct answer."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Review the misconceptions shown in the mock. Distinguish a missing fact from an incorrect rule or weak justification. Explain that a useful correction states what changed and why, then checks understanding through a different example."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model one correction on a parallel question. Trace the mechanism or control rule, link a material property to the brief or replace a vague requirement with a measurable test. Show the evidence that makes the corrected reasoning stronger."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate their original mock, explain significant corrections and complete a new independent question on the same skill. Partners check explanations against the criteria, then each student records a specific action for assessment preparation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare original work, corrections and the new independent attempt. Ask students to explain the reasoning they can now apply and record misconceptions that still require targeted teaching."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the misconception shown in the mock. Provide one worked example, vocabulary guide or structured diagram, then remove the scaffold for the new independent question."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two plausible corrections, justify which better meets the brief and identify a limitation or additional test that would strengthen their answer."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: selected taught Core content sections","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared mock tasks, model responses and feedback criteria","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w8":{"overview":"Students will demonstrate the selected taught design and technology outcomes through independent internal school knowledge and design tasks. They will interpret a brief, use evidence, communicate a reasoned proposal and submit the required test or trace information. The assessment will identify achievement and the particular concepts or design skills needing follow-up.","assessment":"Use the prepared school criteria to assess accurate concepts, relevant evidence, justified decisions and how well the design meets its brief. Collect the original individual responses, annotated drawings or model evidence and test records. Record authorised support and identify specific follow-up learning needs.","lessons":[{"date":"13 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate independent understanding of the taught design and technology concepts.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Distribute the prepared internal school assessment and check the agreed access arrangements. Ask students to read the instructions and identify the response evidence they must submit."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that the assessment covers user needs, product analysis, emerging technologies, lifecycle choices, energy, materials and mechanical devices. Clarify permitted resources, the requirement to justify decisions and the submission arrangements. Questions should assess the selected taught outcomes rather than untaught specialist manufacturing knowledge."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Check access to diagrams, case information and writing materials. Clarify administrative instructions without explaining an assessed mechanism, suggesting a material choice or solving a question for students."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently complete the prepared knowledge, diagram and product-case questions. They label systems accurately, use supplied evidence and justify design decisions against a stated need. They check that their answers address each requirement and submit all response sheets."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect responses and check for missing pages. Assess accurate concepts, use of evidence and justified decisions using the prepared school criteria. Record individual misconceptions for feedback after assessment."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply agreed access arrangements and clarify instructions only. Record authorised assistance without providing assessed terminology, interpreting a diagram or recommending a solution."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same taught criteria, students explain a limitation or alternative solution with supporting evidence. Credit the quality of reasoning rather than additional untaught content."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"14 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to apply the taught design skills independently to a defined school assessment brief.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Issue the individual design brief and check access to the approved paper modelling or drawing materials. Confirm required planning, testing and justification evidence before work begins."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that students must analyse a user need and communicate a suitable proposal using the taught skills. The first assessment uses a simple mechanism or material-based design; the later assessment may include an input-process-output plan, control flowchart and measurable specification. Use the prepared brief and school criteria."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Clarify how the supplied components, diagrams or templates should be used and submitted. Check administrative understanding without demonstrating the assessed design or telling students which decisions to make."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently develop the required annotated design or low-risk paper model. They justify choices against the user need, record the requested test or trace evidence and identify a limitation. They check their evidence against the brief and submit planning, final work and evaluation together."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the complete individual evidence. Assess how well the proposal meets the brief, the accuracy of its explanation and the relevance of its testing. Record the specific design skill requiring further teaching."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Use agreed accessible materials and recorded administrative assistance. Keep assessed design decisions individual. Use paper diagrams where equivalent to the brief rather than introducing unfamiliar equipment during assessment."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify a trade-off between feasible alternatives or explain how a changed condition would affect their proposal, staying within the same brief and assessment criteria."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: selected taught Core content sections","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared internal school assessment tasks and individual design briefs","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w10":{"overview":"Students will identify input, process and output in simple electronic systems. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will plan a simple control system with a clear decision rule and test cases. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"27 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to identify input, process and output in simple electronic systems.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a diagram of an automatic night light. Ask how it can respond to darkness and why a battery alone cannot decide when the lamp should switch on."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce a system as linked input, process and output functions, with a power supply providing energy. Explain sensors, a decision or processing stage and outputs such as light, sound or motion. Distinguish the information from a sensor from the energy needed by the output."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Annotate a block diagram for a light-controlled lamp. Label the light sensor as input, the threshold decision as process and the lamp as output. Model daytime and nighttime cases and explain that the physical circuit must be designed to implement the intended rule."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students classify components in three teacher-prepared product diagrams and draw an input-process-output model for one. They describe what happens when the input changes and identify the power source separately. A peer checks whether the process is an actual rule rather than simply the word computer."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students why the sensor is not the output and what the processor must decide. Check the block diagrams and use a contrasting temperature-controlled example to test transfer."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide component pictures, colour-coded blocks and an example rule. Complete one diagram together, then remove the labels for a different product."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify an example with two inputs and explain how combining them could alter the output, stating the decision rule clearly."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"28 October 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to plan a simple control system with a clear decision rule and test cases.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Ask whether a classroom warning device should sound whenever any movement occurs. Use the responses to show why the intended context must be defined before choosing inputs."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Set a brief for a model desk-light controller that turns a light on when the room is dark. Explain system boundaries, threshold conditions and expected output states. Keep the design at block-diagram or simulation level; no mains wiring or unknown circuits are needed."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a sensor reading below a stated threshold and trace the resulting lamp state. Add test cases below, exactly at and above the threshold. Explain why the boundary case depends on the chosen comparison and must be specified rather than guessed."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students plan an input-process-output system for the supplied brief, write its decision rule and produce at least three test cases with expected outputs. Partners trace the rule and identify an ambiguous condition. Students revise the rule and explain how the correction affects a test case."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check that the rule and expected outputs agree, particularly at the boundary. Ask what happens when the condition is false and identify plans that leave that state undefined."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a partially completed block diagram and a table of sensor readings. Begin with two clear states before adding the exact threshold case."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students propose a second input or a delay to address an unwanted response, explaining how the added condition would change their test plan."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.6 Electronic systems","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w11":{"overview":"Students will explain how a programmable component follows stored instructions to control a system. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will model a control algorithm with a flowchart and verify it using test cases. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"3 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to explain how a programmable component follows stored instructions to control a system.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare a fixed switch with a controller that can follow different instructions. Ask what must change if a designer wants a different response without replacing the whole product."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Use the programmable components section to introduce a microcontroller as a component that runs stored instructions, reads inputs and controls outputs. Distinguish the hardware, program and external components. Explain sequence, selection and repetition through familiar control examples."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Trace a teacher-prepared controller that reads a button and switches an indicator on while pressed. Identify the input value, decision, output and repeated check. Show that changing the decision rule changes behaviour while the diagram of the main components can remain the same."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students label a controller diagram and trace two short instruction sequences against supplied input values. They predict outputs, identify a missing repeated check and explain the difference between changing hardware and changing the program."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students why instructions must be unambiguous and why checking an input only once may be inadequate. Collect a trace to check that the predicted output follows the stored rule."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide hardware-program-input-output cards and a step-by-step trace table. Physically act out one cycle before students trace another input independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify a case where changing the program is insufficient because a different sensor or output device is needed, and justify that distinction."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"4 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to model a control algorithm with a flowchart and verify it using test cases.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display a flowchart whose decision has only a Yes path. Ask what happens when the input does not meet the condition and how that omission affects the product."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce start/end, process, input/output and decision symbols and the use of labelled arrows. Explain that a flowchart must communicate the intended sequence and all possible decision outcomes. A loop repeats checks; the model still needs defined behaviour when a condition is false."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Draw a flowchart for the dark-room lamp rule from the previous week. Trace below, equal-to and above-threshold inputs, including a change from dark to bright. Model correcting a missing off instruction and show how a retest confirms the correction."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students construct a control flowchart for a supplied sensor-and-output brief. They trace at least four cases, including a boundary and a changing input, record expected and observed model outputs and correct one inconsistency. Use paper traces or an available classroom simulation; save the finished chart and evidence."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask a partner to follow the chart without verbal help. Check decision labels, complete paths and whether the repeated check allows a changed input to update the output."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide symbol cards, an incomplete chart and a test table. Build the first decision together and use arrows that can be moved before committing to a final drawing."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students add an explicit second condition and devise a test that distinguishes the revised algorithm from the original, explaining the extra complexity."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.7 The use of programmable components","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w12":{"overview":"Students will classify material families and relate working properties to product use. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will compare paper, polymers, metals and timbers for a defined product component. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"10 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to classify material families and relate working properties to product use.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present paper, a polymer item, a metal spoon and a timber offcut or photographs. Ask why appearance alone may not reveal a material’s properties or how it can be worked."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce the book’s core material families: metals, papers and boards, polymers, fibres and textiles, and natural and manufactured timbers. Explain physical properties and working properties through stiffness, toughness, hardness, conductivity, formability and ease of joining. Clarify that properties vary between specific materials within a family."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Compare a paperboard package with a polymer container. Identify the actual material where known, explain folding versus moulding and connect the choice to the product. Model qualifying an uncertain identification rather than claiming that every similar-looking material behaves alike."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students create a family-property-use table from teacher-prepared samples and information cards. They identify each family, record two relevant properties and explain a suitable use. They distinguish an observation they made from a property supplied by the data card."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to distinguish stiffness from strength and a physical property from a working consideration. Check that examples use specific material evidence rather than treating all metals or polymers as identical."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a glossary and a table with family names already entered. Use touch-safe prepared samples and match one property to one use before requiring two linked explanations."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify an exception within a family and explain why the exact material grade and product form should be specified before manufacture."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"11 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to compare paper, polymers, metals and timbers for a defined product component.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Present a desk organiser brief and ask whether the cheapest sheet material necessarily produces the lowest-cost durable product. Invite students to consider making, joining and expected use."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain comparison through a common set of criteria: stiffness, mass, durability, moisture resistance, ease of shaping, joining and cost. Introduce ferrous and non-ferrous metals, thermoforming and thermosetting polymers, and natural and manufactured timbers at recognition level using the book’s selected examples."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a comparison of card, a named polymer sheet, aluminium and plywood using supplied evidence. Explain why card may suit a prototype while another material better suits repeated use. Distinguish choosing a model material from specifying a final production material."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete a four-material comparison for a stationery holder and recommend separate materials for a prototype and a finished product. They explain two decisive criteria, one rejected option and how a manufacturing or joining constraint affects the recommendation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask what changes if the holder must withstand damp conditions or be easily disassembled. Check that the revised selection responds to the changed need and still uses the evidence provided."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Offer a comparison grid, material cards and highlighted essential criteria. Compare two choices first, then use the same questions for the remaining materials."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students propose a small comparative test with controlled dimensions and loading, explain its limits and identify why a single test cannot establish every property."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.8–1.12 Material families and properties","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w13":{"overview":"Students will research a design context and distinguish user evidence from assumptions. They will develop their understanding through a teacher explanation, a worked example and an evidence-based classroom task. In the second lesson, students will write a measurable design specification supported by user research. Their recorded reasoning, practical evidence and final checks will show whether they can apply the ideas to a defined user, product or design problem.","assessment":"Use teacher questioning, labelled diagrams, case comparisons and the completed independent tasks. Check accurate terminology, evidence linked to the user or brief and a reasoned explanation of choices. Review recorded tests or traces alongside conclusions, and use the final check in both lessons to identify the next skill needing practice.","lessons":[{"date":"17 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to research a design context and distinguish user evidence from assumptions.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show a photograph of a cluttered shared desk and ask whether designing a larger storage box is already justified. List what is visible and what still needs to be asked."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Introduce context research through observation, product analysis and respectful user questions. Explain primary and secondary evidence, recording findings accurately and avoiding leading questions. Use a manageable school stationery context; collect no unnecessary personal information."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model a short interview with a fictional user. Replace Do you want a bigger box with What is difficult when finding your stationery. Record a quoted need, an observed constraint and an unanswered question, then convert the findings into a focused problem statement."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Pairs plan three neutral questions, role-play a user interview using a supplied profile and analyse an existing product. Each student records findings in an evidence table, distinguishes assumptions and writes a design problem supported by at least two pieces of evidence."},{"label":"C:","text":"Check whether the problem statement identifies a user, difficulty and context without prescribing the final solution. Ask which question would most reduce the remaining uncertainty."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a fictional user profile, interview stems and an evidence-assumption table. Model rewriting a leading question and allow oral rehearsal before the interview."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify conflicting findings and propose a further observation or question that could clarify which need should be prioritised."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"18 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to write a measurable design specification supported by user research.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Compare must look nice with must fit within the measured desk area. Ask which allows a finished product to be checked fairly and what evidence is needed to set a sensible limit."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain a specification as a set of justified requirements derived from research. Distinguish essential requirements from preferences and measurable criteria from vague statements. Cover function, dimensions, user access, materials, stability and appropriate classroom constraints without copying unsupported numbers."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Convert a fictional user’s need to store six supplied pencils into a requirement and an acceptance test. Use the measured desk space to set a maximum footprint. Model a table with requirement, research reason, priority and test, and explain why decorative preferences need clearer criteria."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students write six specification points for their researched stationery context, including at least four objectively testable requirements. They link each point to evidence and propose how it will be checked. Partners identify a vague or conflicting point and students revise it before submitting the specification."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask students to demonstrate how a proposed test would produce a pass or fail for one essential requirement. Check that numerical limits come from measurement or the supplied brief and that criteria are achievable within the classroom context."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide a specification table and examples of vague and measurable requirements. Help students convert one research finding into a testable point before they complete the remaining points independently."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students rank conflicting requirements, justify a trade-off and explain how the specification could support later concept selection without dictating a single design."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: 1.13 Contexts which inform design outcomes; 1.16 Design strategies; 1.17 Developing and communicating design ideas","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared analysis worksheet, comparison grid or test-recording table","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w14":{"overview":"Students will consolidate the taught design and technology concepts through an independent mock and a structured review. They will identify misconceptions, explain corrections and apply the learning to a new question or design decision. The two lessons will make individual strengths and remaining preparation needs clear before the internal school assessment.","assessment":"Compare original mock responses, annotated corrections and new independent work. Check whether students can explain the cause of an error and transfer the corrected reasoning to another case. Record one focused preparation need for each student.","lessons":[{"date":"24 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to complete a mock assessment and identify gaps in the taught design skills.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Display a short product recommendation with no evidence. Ask what a reader would need to judge it. Explain that the mock will identify which concepts and explanations require further practice."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Outline the mock coverage: user needs, product analysis, emerging technologies, lifecycle choices, energy, materials and mechanical devices, electronic systems, programmable control and measurable specifications. Explain the criteria for accurate terminology, labelled diagrams, evidence and justified decisions. Keep the original independent response separate from later supported corrections so progress remains visible."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Read the task instructions and evidence requirements together without solving the mock questions. Clarify how diagrams, a short product case and a planning task should be completed and submitted."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students complete the mock independently, including concept questions, a labelled system or mechanism diagram and a justified design decision. They mark uncertain responses and check their work against the instructions. Collect the original work and identify whether each difficulty concerns knowledge, reasoning, diagram accuracy or meeting the brief."},{"label":"C:","text":"Ask each student to identify one secure skill and one uncertain answer. Use the mock evidence to select anonymous examples and group the learning needs for the next lesson."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Provide readable diagrams, clear instructions and agreed classroom access support. During subsequent feedback, target the specific learning need while preserving the original independent work."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students identify an alternative solution and explain when it would be preferable. Ask them to test whether their original justification still holds if a key user requirement changes."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"25 November 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to use mock feedback to correct a design response and apply the learning to a new task.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Show an anonymous mock error such as a reversed gear direction, a vague specification or an unsupported material choice. Ask how the error could be identified rather than simply supplying the correct answer."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Review the misconceptions shown in the mock. Distinguish a missing fact from an incorrect rule or weak justification. Explain that a useful correction states what changed and why, then checks understanding through a different example."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Model one correction on a parallel question. Trace the mechanism or control rule, link a material property to the brief or replace a vague requirement with a measurable test. Show the evidence that makes the corrected reasoning stronger."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students annotate their original mock, explain significant corrections and complete a new independent question on the same skill. Partners check explanations against the criteria, then each student records a specific action for assessment preparation."},{"label":"C:","text":"Compare original work, corrections and the new independent attempt. Ask students to explain the reasoning they can now apply and record misconceptions that still require targeted teaching."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Group students by the misconception shown in the mock. Provide one worked example, vocabulary guide or structured diagram, then remove the scaffold for the new independent question."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students compare two plausible corrections, justify which better meets the brief and identify a limitation or additional test that would strengthen their answer."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: selected taught Core content sections","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared mock tasks, model responses and feedback criteria","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders; confirm Loy Krathong arrangements on Tuesday 24 November and reschedule any displaced task"]},"y10-11-design-technology-t1-w15":{"overview":"Students will demonstrate the selected taught design and technology outcomes through independent internal school knowledge and design tasks. They will interpret a brief, use evidence, communicate a reasoned proposal and submit the required test or trace information. The assessment will identify achievement and the particular concepts or design skills needing follow-up.","assessment":"Use the prepared school criteria to assess accurate concepts, relevant evidence, justified decisions and how well the design meets its brief. Collect the original individual responses, annotated drawings or model evidence and test records. Record authorised support and identify specific follow-up learning needs.","lessons":[{"date":"1 December 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to demonstrate independent understanding of the taught design and technology concepts.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Distribute the prepared internal school assessment and check the agreed access arrangements. Ask students to read the instructions and identify the response evidence they must submit."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that the assessment covers user needs, product analysis, emerging technologies, lifecycle choices, energy, materials and mechanical devices, electronic systems, programmable control and measurable specifications. Clarify permitted resources, the requirement to justify decisions and the submission arrangements. Questions should assess the selected taught outcomes rather than untaught specialist manufacturing knowledge."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Check access to diagrams, case information and writing materials. Clarify administrative instructions without explaining an assessed mechanism, suggesting a material choice or solving a question for students."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently complete the prepared knowledge, diagram and product-case questions. They label systems accurately, use supplied evidence and justify design decisions against a stated need. They check that their answers address each requirement and submit all response sheets."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect responses and check for missing pages. Assess accurate concepts, use of evidence and justified decisions using the prepared school criteria. Record individual misconceptions for feedback after assessment."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Apply agreed access arrangements and clarify instructions only. Record authorised assistance without providing assessed terminology, interpreting a diagram or recommending a solution."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Within the same taught criteria, students explain a limitation or alternative solution with supporting evidence. Credit the quality of reasoning rather than additional untaught content."}],"holiday":false},{"date":"2 December 2026","objective":"By the end of this lesson, students will be able to apply the taught design skills independently to a defined school assessment brief.","flow":[{"label":"H:","text":"Issue the individual design brief and check access to the approved paper modelling or drawing materials. Confirm required planning, testing and justification evidence before work begins."},{"label":"ID:","text":"Explain that students must analyse a user need and communicate a suitable proposal using the taught skills. The first assessment uses a simple mechanism or material-based design; the later assessment may include an input-process-output plan, control flowchart and measurable specification. Use the prepared brief and school criteria."},{"label":"WD:","text":"Clarify how the supplied components, diagrams or templates should be used and submitted. Check administrative understanding without demonstrating the assessed design or telling students which decisions to make."},{"label":"YD:","text":"Students independently develop the required annotated design or low-risk paper model. They justify choices against the user need, record the requested test or trace evidence and identify a limitation. They check their evidence against the brief and submit planning, final work and evaluation together."},{"label":"C:","text":"Collect the complete individual evidence. Assess how well the proposal meets the brief, the accuracy of its explanation and the relevance of its testing. Record the specific design skill requiring further teaching."}],"differentiation":[{"label":"Support:","text":"Use agreed accessible materials and recorded administrative assistance. Keep assessed design decisions individual. Use paper diagrams where equivalent to the brief rather than introducing unfamiliar equipment during assessment."},{"label":"Extension:","text":"Students justify a trade-off between feasible alternatives or explain how a changed condition would affect their proposal, staying within the same brief and assessment criteria."}],"holiday":false}],"materials":["Pearson Edexcel GCSE (9–1) Design and Technology revision notes: selected taught Core content sections","Teacher computer, display screen and whiteboard markers","Prepared product scenarios, diagrams and user briefs aligned to the lesson objectives","Prepared internal school assessment tasks and individual design briefs","Paper, pencils, rulers, card templates and approved classroom stationery","Teacher-prepared samples or component cards; pre-cut model parts where required","Lesson success criteria, observation checklist and labelled evidence folders"]}});
